From: Remora <noreply@localhost>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 09:56:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] VideoFramePool: bound the surplus-buffer drop retry

shouldDropBuffer() rejects any block whose buffer id is not already in
mBuffers once that set has reached mMaxBufferCount, and getVideoFrameTask()
turns the rejection into C2_TIMED_OUT and reposts itself. mBuffers is
insert-only -- nothing ever erases from it -- and the repost has no retry
limit, so the rejection is permanent: no callback is ever delivered, no error
is raised, and the decoder simply stops.

The bookkeeping assumes a pool that recycles a fixed set of buffer ids for its
lifetime. A BUFFERQUEUE pool does exactly that, which is why surface-backed
playback never sees this. A bufferpool-backed one does not: the id is
BufferPoolData::mId, minted per allocation, so as soon as the whitelist is
full every freshly allocated buffer is unknown and is dropped forever.

Measured on a no-surface (ByteBuffer) MediaCodec consumer: output stopped dead
at exactly mMaxBufferCount frames -- 25 for h264 and hevc, 17 for vp9, each
equal to DecodeInterface::getOutputDelay(codec) + 9 -- with the input pool
fully held and no diagnostic of any kind. Both c2-va decode paths were
affected; c2.android.avc.decoder on the identical harness ran to completion,
which is what localised it here rather than in the component.

Take a surplus buffer after kMaxDropRetries rather than retrying forever, and
LATCH that discovery. The latch is not an optimisation, it is the difference
between a fix and a regression: bounding alone left every subsequent frame
re-paying 16 backoff retries capped at 16ms, which fired 31 times in a
60-frame decode and made hardware decode 8x SLOWER than the software codec
(8.4s vs 1.0s for 60 frames). Once the pool has been observed minting an
unrecognised id the whitelist can never match again, so keeping it costs delay
and buys nothing. With the latch the penalty is paid once per pool: the same
clip decodes in ~1.28s, 60/60 frames byte-identical to a software reference,
and the accept path fires exactly once.

A BUFFERQUEUE pool never reaches the bound, so surface playback keeps the
original behaviour untouched.

bd remora-e5x.38
---
diff --git a/components/VideoFramePool.cpp b/components/VideoFramePool.cpp
index fe616cb..39bf320 100644
--- a/components/VideoFramePool.cpp
+++ b/components/VideoFramePool.cpp
@@ -158,6 +158,12 @@ void VideoFramePool::destroyTask() {
 }
 
 bool VideoFramePool::shouldDropBuffer(uint32_t bufferId) {
+    // Whitelisting only makes sense against a pool that recycles a fixed set of ids. Once we have
+    // proof this one does not, every further drop is delay that ends in accepting the buffer
+    // anyway.
+    if (mIdsAreNotRecycled) {
+        return false;
+    }
     if (mBuffers.size() < mMaxBufferCount) {
         return false;
     }
@@ -202,6 +208,9 @@ void VideoFramePool::getVideoFrameTask() {
     constexpr size_t kFetchRetryDelayInit = 256;   // Initial delay: 256us
     constexpr size_t kFetchRetryDelayMax = 16384;  // Max delay: 16ms (1 frame at 60fps)
     constexpr size_t kFenceWaitTimeoutNs = 16000000;  // 16ms (1 frame at 60fps)
+    // How many surplus-buffer drops to tolerate before taking one anyway. Reached only when the
+    // block pool keeps minting unrecognised ids; a BUFFERQUEUE pool never gets here.
+    constexpr size_t kMaxDropRetries = 16;
     static size_t sNumRetries = 0;
     static size_t sDelay = kFetchRetryDelayInit;
 
@@ -245,12 +254,32 @@ void VideoFramePool::getVideoFrameTask() {
         if (bufferId) {
             ALOGV("%s(): Got buffer with id = %u", __func__, *bufferId);
 
-            if (shouldDropBuffer(*bufferId)) {
+            if (shouldDropBuffer(*bufferId) && sNumRetries < kMaxDropRetries) {
                 // We drop buffer, since we got more then needed.
                 ALOGV("%s(): Dropping allocated buffer with id = %u", __func__, *bufferId);
                 bufferId = std::nullopt;
                 block.reset();
                 err = C2_TIMED_OUT;
+            } else if (!mIdsAreNotRecycled && shouldDropBuffer(*bufferId)) {
+                // BOUNDED, and it must be (bd remora-e5x.38). mBuffers is insert-only and capped at
+                // mMaxBufferCount, so it is a LIFETIME whitelist of buffer ids, not a concurrency
+                // limit. That holds for a BUFFERQUEUE pool, which recycles a fixed set of slots
+                // whose ids never change. It does NOT hold for a bufferpool-backed one — the id is
+                // BufferPoolData::mId, minted per allocation — so once the whitelist is full every
+                // newly allocated buffer is unknown, gets dropped here, and the retry below reposts
+                // forever: no callback, no error, no bound. The decoder simply stops, at exactly
+                // mMaxBufferCount frames (measured: 25 for h264/hevc, 17 for vp9, each equal to
+                // getOutputDelay + 9). Accepting a surplus buffer costs one extra allocation and
+                // guarantees forward progress; hanging costs the stream.
+                // Latch, so this is paid ONCE per pool rather than per frame. Measured before
+                // latching: the accept path fired 31 times in a 60-frame decode, each after 16
+                // backoff retries, and hardware decode ran 8x SLOWER than the software codec
+                // (8.4s vs 1.0s for 60 frames). Correct but useless is not a fix.
+                mIdsAreNotRecycled = true;
+                ALOGW("%s(): accepting unlisted buffer id = %u after %zu drops; the block pool is "
+                      "minting new ids rather than recycling a fixed set — disabling the whitelist "
+                      "for this pool",
+                      __func__, *bufferId, sNumRetries);
             }
         }
     }
diff --git a/components/include/v4l2_codec2/components/VideoFramePool.h b/components/include/v4l2_codec2/components/VideoFramePool.h
index 6bebc37..e830868 100644
--- a/components/include/v4l2_codec2/components/VideoFramePool.h
+++ b/components/include/v4l2_codec2/components/VideoFramePool.h
@@ -73,6 +73,11 @@ private:
     size_t mMaxBufferCount;
     // Contains known buffer ids that are valid for the pool.
     std::set<uint32_t> mBuffers;
+    // Latched once the block pool has been observed minting a buffer id that is not in mBuffers
+    // after the whitelist is full. That means the ids are per-allocation rather than a recycled
+    // fixed set, so the whitelist can never match again and dropping is pure delay — ~200ms of
+    // backoff per frame, measured, on more than half the frames of a 60-frame clip.
+    bool mIdsAreNotRecycled = false;
 
     const ui::Size mSize;
     const HalPixelFormat mPixelFormat;
